{"concepts_used":["GC-0075","GC-0110","GC-0064","GC-0093","GC-0070"],"decline_reason":"","declined":false,"citations":[{"ref":"TMM/Part12/8#1","quote":"Before accepting, or recommending acceptance of, an application where a citation has lapsed or has been amended to delete the conflicting goods and/or services, it is always necessary to check whether a divisional application based on a citation has been filed."},{"ref":"TMM/Part12/8#1","quote":"If a divisional application based on a cited mark has been filed, late grounds for rejection must be raised based on the divisional application against the application under examination if the goods and/or services are still similar to the subject trade mark."}],"answer":"Yes—in the circumstances specified by the Manual. Under Manual practice, before accepting or recommending acceptance where a citation has lapsed or been amended to delete conflicting goods or services, the examiner must check whether a divisional application based on that citation has been filed [TMM/Part12/8#1].

The Manual also says that, if such a divisional has been filed and the goods or services are still similar, late grounds for rejection must be raised based on the divisional [TMM/Part12/8#1]. These are statements of Manual practice, not quotations from legislation.

The supplied passages do not address the effect of a design registration or establish a design-registration exception to that check. I therefore cannot confirm any design-specific rule from these materials; the answer above explains the general examination practice, not how your particular application would be decided."}
